<h1>Hello from a .hd file</h1><p>This is a body-only HTML fragment — no <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, or <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> wrappers. Just semantic content.</p><h2>Why this matters</h2><p>Markdown can't easily express things like the table below — merged cells, multi-line content, or inline formatting inside cells. HTML can. The <code>.hd</code> extension signals "documentation HTML fragment" rather than a full HTML page.</p><h2>A table that markdown can't cleanly render</h2><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Feature</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markdown</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>.hd</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Basic tables</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Yes</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Yes</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Merged cells (colspan/rowspan)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Yes</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Multi-paragraph cells</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Yes</strong> — like this<br><br>Second paragraph in the same cell.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" rowspan="1"><p><em>Merged across two columns →</em></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Works fine</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>A list</h2><ul><li><p>Headings, paragraphs, lists, links — all standard HTML</p></li><li><p>No <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags allowed in the document</p></li><li><p>Assets stored alongside the file in a sibling folder (TBD convention)</p></li></ul><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://claude.com/blog/using-claude-code-the-unreasonable-effectiveness-of-html">Reference: Claude blog post on HTML over Markdown</a></p>